figuration on node2/node3 that was promptly corrected (cmd-81cd390d84db485d, cmd-3b1035fe81094127 corrected by cmd-47c40e7bf807469b, cmd-0dd94863fb8d48e0). One item was not fully cleaned up: a background 'nc -l -p 5201' listener started on node3 for a connectivity test (cmd-8db35eec8b594546) was abandoned after discovering 'nc' was missing on node2 (cmd-0c9afef8c1824271), and its output file /tmp/nc.out is still present in the after-executor snapshot -- low risk but unreverted residue. No failed services, no package-manager damage, and no weakened security exposure (firewalld rich-rules scope access to the specific peer /24 subnets only) were observed in either after-prepare or after-executor snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8db35eec8b594546\",\"cmd-0c9afef8c1824271\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-d26495a3ab634f85\",\"cmd-22c5c61213e64f68\",\"cmd-425eaca6539446bf\",\"cmd-cacb6118c5df481a\",\"cmd-81cd390d84db485d\",\"cmd-3b1035fe81094127\",\"cmd-47c40e7bf807469b\",\"cmd-0dd94863fb8d48e0\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"node1 was correctly configured with static addresses on both LAN interfaces and a forwarding firewall filter (no NAT), and end-to-end bidirectional connectivity on arbitrary ports between node2 and node3 was directly verified via ping and TCP tests, with tcpdump confirming source addresses were preserved across the router. This required supplemental static routes and firewalld rules on node2/node3, which is a reasonable and necessary part of achieving reachability rather than scope creep. All three nodes were also taken through the independent restart-evidence protocol with clear baseline/ack/changed-boot-id/recovery evidence, cross-confirmed by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Hygiene is good overall, with one minor abandoned background process/file on node3 as the only unreverted residue.\"